Why Do We Use Lag?

Time series assumes:

"Past values influence future values."

Example:

Today's stock price is related to yesterday's stock price.

Today's temperature is related to yesterday's temperature.

Today's sales are related to yesterday's sales.

=================

| Day   | Value | Lag 1 | Difference |
| ----- | ----: | ----: | ---------: |
| Day 1 |   200 |    NA |         NA |
| Day 2 |   220 |   200 |         20 |
| Day 3 |   215 |   220 |         -5 |
| Day 4 |   230 |   215 |         15 |

This differencing often removes trends and helps convert non-stationary data into stationary data.

-----------------

Lag is a previous observation in a time series. Lag 1 refers to the immediately previous value, Lag 2 refers to the value two time periods earlier, 
and so on. Lags are used in forecasting models and ADF tests to understand how past values influence current values.



